xnu 0412: a submission writes back the buffers the client marks as CPU-written

A home-screen frame makes 11-17 submissions and 3.4-5.9 ms of it is inside
them, 260-300 us each (`mondrian-gl: submits:`), while the ring clean and the
doorbell cost 370 ns. The submission is not the GPU: `MondrianGPU: wrote back
16 of 22 buffers (12496 KiB), 371 us` is the user client writing back every
mapped buffer, because a mapping is the only evidence it has that the CPU
*wrote* it. freedreno's mappings persist and fd_bo_cpu_prep returns without an
ioctl for a buffer it does not share, so 0221's exact rule had to fall back to
"mapped means written" and 0252 could only cap it by size.

The client can say it exactly: Mesa marks each buffer the CPU wrote -- the
upload path, writable transfer maps and flushes, the driver's own control,
border-colour, descriptor, vertex-parameter and warm-up buffers -- and the
submission that carries the buffer sets MSM_SUBMIT_BO_CPU_WRITE. The mark
rides on the submission, so no per-write ioctl is needed, and the submission
after the write writes back those buffers and the kernel's own dirty ones, and
holds the rest: the home screen's fourth submission becomes 3 of 25 buffers
(8208 KiB) in 193-234 us instead of 16 of 22 (12496 KiB) in 371 us. A counter,
not a flag, keeps a write that races the submit from being cleared without
going out (tools/mesa/mondrian-gpu-writes.patch).

The buffer goes back whole, not by the byte range the client wrote. The range
was tried: with (offset << 32) | length in `presumed`, the fourth submission
announced 25 KiB and the boot stopped at the black spinner (`present 0`,
`uc retired 28 of 76`, `fault 0x0`). Two writers that both find the counter at
zero reset the recorded range and one of them is lost, and the ring heap
suballocates several of them into one 4 MiB block, so the range describes
whoever marked last instead of everyone. A range that misses one write is a
command stream the CP executes stale, which parks it. The announcement is what
removes the 12.5 MiB; the bytes it saves on top did not reach the frame anyway
(see the paragraph above: 4.3 MiB less is 0.3 ms of a 3.4-5.9 ms budget).

An adopted IOSurface is the one buffer whose writer is not this client: an app
locks it, writes the pixels, unlocks. MondrianSurface already bumps a seed on
every write unlock, so surfaceBuffer hands the seed to bufferAdopt and a
re-adoption of pixels nobody wrote no longer dirties the whole surface: the
4.5 MB surfaces the window server re-adopts every frame were written back on
every submission before. Memory with no seed (a process's own pages) keeps
the conservative rule, and gpusynclog=1 names each buffer of the fourth
submission with its size, flags and seed.

gpusyncwrites=1 switches the rule on; without it the mapped rule stands, so a
boot that reads a buffer stale has one argument to blame and one to remove.
gpusyncall=1 still writes everything back.
